l</guid><description/></item><item><title>Data Binding Exercise</title><link>https://textbooks.cs.ksu.edu/cis400/c-exercises-s23/08-data-binding-exercise/index.html</link><pubDate>Fri, 24 Aug 2018 10:53:26 -0500</pubDate><guid>https://textbooks.cs.ksu.edu/cis400/c-exercises-s23/08-data-binding-exercise/index.html</guid><description/></item><item><title>Dependency Property Exercise</title><link>https://textbooks.cs.ksu.edu/cis400/c-exercises-s23/09-dependency-property-exercise/index.html</link><pubDate>Fri, 24 Aug 2018 10:53:26 -0500</pubDate><guid>https://textbooks.cs.ksu.edu/cis400/c-exercises-s23/09-dependency-property-exercise/index.html</guid><description/></item><item><title>Razor Pages Exercise</title><link>https://textbooks.cs.ksu.edu/cis400/c-exercises-s23/10-razor-page-exercise/index.html</link><pubDate>Fri, 24 Aug 2018 10:53:26 -0500</pubDate><guid>https://textbooks.cs.ksu.edu/cis400/c-exercises-s23/10-razor-page-exercise/index.html</guid><description>Now that you know how to create Razor pages, let’s see what makes them useful for creating dynamic web pages.
Initial Project We’ll start with a simple ASP.NET web app using Razor Pages to display a database of movies. This app consists of a single page, Index that will be used to display the details of the movies in the database. It also contains classes representing an individual movie (Movie) and the movie database (MovieDatabase).</description></item><item><title>Responsive Design</title><link>https://textbooks.cs.ksu.edu/cis400/c-exercises-s23/11-responsive-design/index.html</link><pubDate>Fri, 24 Aug 2018 10:53:05 -0500</pubDate><guid>https://textbooks.cs.ksu.edu/cis400/c-exercises-s23/11-responsive-design/index.html</guid><description>Fitting the Screen</description></item><item><title>Web Data</title><link>https://textbooks.cs.ksu.edu/cis400/c-exercises-s23/12-web-data/index.html</link><pubDate>Fri, 24 Aug 2018 10:53:05 -0500</pubDate><guid>https://textbooks.cs.ksu.edu/cis400/c-exercises-s23/12-web-data/index.html</guid><description>Now that we’ve seen how to dynamically create the content of a Razor Page on the server, we should turn our attention to how we can do so based on our user’s needs. Consider the example application we have been developing - it exposes a database of movie information to the user. How might a user want to use this database? They might want to find the details for a specific movie - who directed it, when was it released, etc. They might be looking for a movie they want to watch in a favorite genre. They might be looking to find all the movies directed by a favorite director… there are a lot of possibilities.</description></item><item><title>Deployment</title><link>https://textbooks.cs.ksu.edu/cis400/c-exercises-s23/13-deployment/index.html</link><pubDate>Fri, 24 Aug 2018 10:53:05 -0500</pubDate><guid>https://textbooks.cs.ksu.edu/cis400/c-exercises-s23/13-deployment/index.html</guid><description>Now that we have a well-functioning web application, we are ready to publish it to a production environment, where eveyrone on the web will have access to it! This exercise walks you through deploying your new application using Microsoft’s Azure Web Services, utilizing the built-in support for this deployment process in Visual Studio.</description></item></channel></rss>